      "comment": "Formats output to a fixed-size character buffer with size limit protection.\n\nStandard C library snprintf implementation that writes formatted output to a\ndestination buffer, ensuring null-termination and preventing buffer overflow.\nUses an internal FILE structure to manage the formatting process.\n\nAlgorithm:\n1. Initialize local FILE structure with buffer pointer and count\n2. Set file flags to 0x42 (internal string buffer mode)\n3. Call FormatOutputToStream to perform variadic formatting\n4. If destination buffer is non-null:\n   a. Decrement remaining count\n   b. If count underflows (buffer full), call __flsbuf to flush\n   c. Otherwise, null-terminate the output string\n5. Return the number of characters written (excluding null)\n\nParameters:\n  _Dest    (char *)  - Pointer to destination buffer for formatted string\n  _Count   (size_t)  - Maximum number of bytes to write including null terminator\n  _Format  (char *)  - Printf-style format string\n  ...                - Variable arguments matching format specifiers\n\nReturns:\n  int - Number of characters written (excluding null terminator)\n        Returns count of characters that would have been written if buffer\n        was large enough (standard snprintf behavior)\n\nMagic Numbers:\n  0x42 - FILE._flag value indicating string buffer mode (_IOWRT | _IOSTRG)\n\nError Handling:\n  - Null destination buffer skips null-termination but still formats\n  - Buffer overflow handled via __flsbuf flush mechanism",
