      "comment": "Copies nSize bytes from pSrc to pDst, handling overlapping memory regions correctly.\n\nAlgorithm:\n1. Compare destination and source addresses to determine copy direction\n2. If pDst <= pSrc or pDst >= pSrc + nSize, copy forward (low to high addresses)\n3. If regions overlap with pDst > pSrc, copy backward (high to low addresses)\n4. Align destination pointer to DWORD boundary for optimal performance\n5. Copy bulk data using REP MOVSD (4 bytes per iteration, 8 DWORDs unrolled)\n6. Copy remaining 0-3 trailing bytes individually\n7. Return original destination pointer\n\nParameters:\npDst (void*): Destination buffer pointer where data will be copied to\npSrc (void*): Source buffer pointer from which data will be copied  \nnSize (size_t): Number of bytes to copy from source to destination\n\nReturns:\nvoid* - Returns the original pDst pointer (enables function chaining)\n        Never returns NULL; behavior undefined if buffers are invalid\n\nSpecial Cases:\n- Zero-size copy: Returns pDst immediately with no memory access\n- Non-overlapping regions: Uses fast forward copy path\n- Overlapping with pDst < pSrc: Safe forward copy\n- Overlapping with pDst > pSrc: Uses backward copy to prevent corruption\n\nMagic Numbers Reference:\n0x3 - Alignment mask for DWORD boundary check (address & 3 == 0)\n0x8 - Unroll threshold for bulk DWORD copy loop (32 bytes minimum)\n0x4 - DWORD size in bytes\n\nPerformance Notes:\n- DWORD-aligned copies use REP MOVSD for maximum throughput\n- Unaligned destinations are pre-aligned with 1-3 byte copies\n- 8-DWORD unrolled loop reduces loop overhead for large copies\n- Uses computed jump tables for remainder byte handling",
