      "comment": "Initializes a critical section lock for multi-threaded synchronization.\n\nAlgorithm:\n1. Calculate pointer to lock table entry using nLockNum index\n2. Check if critical section already exists (fast path - return success)\n3. Allocate 24 bytes (0x18) for CRITICAL_SECTION structure\n4. If allocation fails, set errno to ENOMEM (0xC) and return failure\n5. Acquire global MT init lock for thread-safe initialization\n6. Double-check if another thread initialized the lock (race condition guard)\n7. If still uninitialized, call InitializeCriticalSectionAndSpinCount with 4000 spin count\n8. If initialization fails, free memory, set errno, unwind SEH, return failure\n9. Store critical section pointer in lock table\n10. If another thread already initialized, free our allocation (lost race)\n11. Release MT init lock and return success\n\nParameters:\nnLockNum (int): Index into the global lock table (g_lpCriticalSection array)\n                Valid range depends on NUM_LOCKS constant\n\nReturns:\n1 - Success, critical section is initialized and ready\n0 - Failure, errno set to ENOMEM (0xC) - insufficient memory\n\nMagic Numbers:\n0x18 (24)   - sizeof(CRITICAL_SECTION) on Win32\n0xC (12)    - ENOMEM error code\n0xA (10)    - Lock number for MT init lock (protects lock table)\n0xFA0 (4000)- Spin count for InitializeCriticalSectionAndSpinCount\n\nSpecial Cases:\n- Uses double-checked locking pattern for thread safety\n- SEH frame used for cleanup on initialization failure\n- Lock table at lpCriticalSection_6ff7e210 holds all CRT locks\n\nLibrary: Visual Studio 2003 Release CRT",
