      "comment": "Calculates the length of a null-terminated string using optimized DWORD scanning.\n\nAlgorithm:\n1. Check pointer alignment to 4-byte boundary (lpszStr & 3)\n2. If unaligned, scan byte-by-byte until aligned or null found\n3. Once aligned, read 4 bytes at a time into dwCurrentDword\n4. Apply magic constant test: ((dword ^ 0xFFFFFFFF ^ (dword + 0x7EFEFEFF)) & 0x81010100)\n5. If test is zero, no null byte in dword - continue scanning\n6. If test non-zero, potential null found - check each byte position\n7. Return pointer difference as string length\n\nParameters:\nlpszStr (char*) - Pointer to null-terminated string to measure\n\nReturns:\nsize_t - Number of characters before the null terminator\n\nMagic Numbers Reference:\n0x7EFEFEFF - Detects null bytes by causing overflow in high bit of each byte lane\n0x81010100 - Mask to check high bits of all 4 byte lanes\n0x3 - Alignment mask for 4-byte boundary check\n0xFF0000 - Mask to check third byte (offset 2)\n0xFF000000 - Mask to check fourth byte (offset 3)\n\nSpecial Cases:\n- Empty string returns 0\n- Unaligned pointers handled with byte-by-byte scan first\n- Multiple exit points based on which byte contains null",
