      "instructions": ["0x67E1|SUB|ESP, 0x48", "0x67E4|PUSH|EBX", "0x67E5|MOV|EBX, 0x480", "0x67EA|PUSH|EBX", "0x67EB|CALL|0x6ff52e19", "0x67F0|TEST|EAX, EAX", "0x67F2|POP|ECX", "0x67F3|JNZ|0x6ff567fd", "0x67F5|OR|EAX, 0xffffffff", "0x67F8|JMP|0x6ff569da", "0x67FD|MOV|[0x6ffa7160], EAX", "0x6802|MOV|dword ptr [0x6ffa7150], 0x20", "0x680C|LEA|ECX, [EAX + 0x480]", "0x6812|JMP|0x6ff56832", "0x6814|OR|dword ptr [EAX], 0xffffffff"],
      "comment": "Initializes the C Runtime I/O subsystem by allocating file handle tables and setting up stdin, stdout, and stderr.\n\nAlgorithm:\n1. Allocate initial file handle table (0x480 bytes = 32 entries x 36 bytes each)\n2. Initialize each I/O entry with invalid handle (-1), zero ref count, and default flags\n3. Retrieve startup info to check for inherited handles from parent process\n4. If inherited handles exist (cbReserved2 != 0), allocate additional tables as needed\n5. Copy inherited OS handles into the I/O table, initializing critical sections\n6. For the first 3 entries (stdin=0, stdout=1, stderr=2):\n   a. If entry is uninitialized, get the corresponding standard handle\n   b. Query file type to determine console vs pipe vs file\n   c. Set appropriate flags based on file type\n   d. Initialize critical section for thread safety\n7. Call SetHandleCount to inform OS of maximum handles\n\nParameters:\n  None - This function takes no parameters.\n\nReturns:\n  0 on success\n  -1 on memory allocation failure or critical section init failure\n\nSpecial Cases:\n  - If _malloc fails during initial allocation, returns -1 immediately\n  - If _malloc fails during extension table allocation, uses current handle count\n  - Critical section initialization failure returns -1\n\nMagic Numbers:\n  0x480 - Size of one I/O table block (32 entries * 36 bytes)\n  0x24 (36) - Size of one ioinfo entry structure\n  0x20 (32) - Number of entries per table block\n  0x800 (2048) - Maximum inherited handles limit\n  0xfa0 (4000) - Spin count for critical section\n  0xfffffff6 (-10) - STD_INPUT_HANDLE\n  0xfffffff5 (-11) - STD_OUTPUT_HANDLE\n  0xfffffff4 (-12) - STD_ERROR_HANDLE\n\nFlag Bits (ioinfo.osfile field):\n  0x01 - FOPEN (file is open)\n  0x08 - FPIPE (file is pipe)\n  0x40 - FDEV (file is device/console)\n  0x80 - FHIT (file handle was inherited)\n  0x81 - FOPEN | FHIT (combination for std handles)\n\nStructure Layout (ioinfo - 36 bytes per entry):\n  Offset  Size  Field           Description\n  0x00    4     osfhnd          OS file handle (HANDLE)\n  0x04    1     osfile          File flags\n  0x05    1     pipech          Pipe lookahead character (default 0x0a = LF)\n  0x08    4     lockinitflag    Lock initialization count\n  0x0c    24    lock            RTL_CRITICAL_SECTION for thread safety\n\nNote: SSA variables (piVar1, puVar2, etc.) are decompiler-generated temporaries that represent register values at different program points. They cannot be renamed but represent:\n  - piVar1/puVar3: Pointer to current ioinfo entry being processed\n  - puVar2/puVar5: Pointer to extension table entry during initialization\n  - iVar4/iVar7: Return values and loop counters\n  - DVar6: DWORD result from GetFileType/GetStdHandle",
      "tags": ["PROP_LARGE", "STRUCT_Act", "STRUCT_Inventory", "CALLER_1", "PARAM_0", "PROP_LOOPHEAVY", "STRUCT_UnitAny", "CALLEE_5", "STRUCT_Control", "STRUCT_PlayerData"]
