      "comment": "Terminates CRT I/O subsystem by cleaning up all file handle tables.\n\nAlgorithm:\n1. Initialize pointer to file handle table array at g_apFileHandleTable (0x6ffa7160)\n2. Outer loop: Iterate through 64 file handle table pointers (0x6ffa7160 to 0x6ffa7260)\n3. For each non-NULL table pointer:\n   a. Inner loop: Iterate through file handle structures (stride 0x24 = 36 bytes)\n   b. Check if handle has initialized critical section (offset +0x8 != 0)\n   c. If initialized, call DeleteCriticalSection on CRITICAL_SECTION at offset +0xC\n   d. Continue until reaching table end (base + 0x480 = 32 handles max per table)\n4. Deallocate the file handle table memory via DeallocateMemory\n5. Clear the table pointer to NULL\n6. Advance to next table pointer and repeat until all tables processed\n\nParameters:\nNone - uses global g_apFileHandleTable array\n\nReturns:\nvoid - no return value\n\nStructure Layout (File Handle Entry - 0x24 bytes):\nOffset  Size  Field               Type              Description\n0x00    4     dwReserved          uint              Reserved/padding\n0x04    4     dwReserved2         uint              Reserved/padding  \n0x08    4     fCritSecInitialized int               Non-zero if critical section initialized\n0x0C    24    csFileLock          CRITICAL_SECTION  Per-handle synchronization object\n0x24    -     (next entry)        -                 Next file handle in table\n\nMagic Numbers:\n0x480 = 1152 bytes = 32 file handles * 36 bytes per handle (max handles per table)\n0x24  = 36 bytes per file handle structure\n0x100 = 256 bytes = 64 table pointers * 4 bytes (table array size)",
      "tags": ["STRUCT_Inventory", "CALLER_1", "CALLEE_2", "PARAM_0"]
