      "comment": "Seeks to a new file position while holding a file lock (CRT internal).\n\nThis is the locked variant of lseek that assumes the caller already holds\nthe file handle lock. It converts the CRT file descriptor to a Win32 HANDLE,\ncalls SetFilePointer, and on success clears the text-mode EOF flag.\n\nAlgorithm:\n  1. Convert CRT file descriptor to OS file handle via GetOsFileHandle\n  2. If handle is invalid (-1), set errno to EBADF (9) and return -1\n  3. Call SetFilePointer with the distance and move method\n  4. If SetFilePointer fails, retrieve Windows error code via GetLastError\n  5. If no error occurred, clear bit 1 (CTRL-Z seen flag) in file flags\n  6. If error occurred, map Windows error to errno via __dosmaperr\n  7. Return new file position or -1 on error\n\nParameters:\n  nFileDescriptor (dword) - CRT file descriptor index (0-based)\n  lDistanceToMove (long)  - Signed byte offset to seek\n  dwMoveMethod (dword)    - Origin: FILE_BEGIN(0), FILE_CURRENT(1), FILE_END(2)\n\nReturns:\n  Success: New absolute file position (dword)\n  Failure: 0xFFFFFFFF (-1) with errno set:\n    - EBADF (9): Invalid file descriptor\n    - Other: Mapped from Windows error via __dosmaperr\n\nSpecial Cases:\n  - File descriptor uses segmented table: index >> 5 selects segment\n  - Each entry is 0x24 (36) bytes, flags at offset 4\n  - Bit 1 of flags (0x02) is CTRL-Z EOF indicator for text mode\n\nMagic Numbers:\n  0x1F        - Mask for index within segment (32 entries per segment)\n  0x24        - Size of file handle table entry (36 bytes)\n  0x04        - Offset to flags byte within entry\n  0xFD        - Mask to clear bit 1 (CTRL-Z seen flag)\n  0x09        - EBADF errno value (bad file descriptor)",
