      "comment": "Compares two null-terminated strings lexicographically.\n\nStandard C library strcmp implementation with DWORD-aligned optimization.\nThis is a high-performance version that reads 4 bytes at once when the\nfirst string pointer is aligned to a 4-byte boundary.\n\nAlgorithm:\n1. Load string pointers from stack (lpszStr1 -> EDX, lpszStr2 -> ECX)\n2. Test if lpszStr1 is DWORD-aligned using TEST EDX, 0x3\n3. If not aligned, branch to alignment handler (check_alignment)\n   3a. If 1-byte unaligned (TEST EDX, 0x1), compare single byte\n   3b. If 2-byte unaligned (TEST EDX, 0x2), compare WORD (2 bytes)\n   3c. After alignment achieved, fall through to DWORD loop\n4. DWORD-aligned loop (aligned_loop):\n   4a. Load 4 bytes from lpszStr1 into EAX\n   4b. Compare byte 0 (AL) with lpszStr2[0], exit if mismatch\n   4c. Check for null terminator in byte 0, exit if found\n   4d. Compare byte 1 (AH) with lpszStr2[1], exit if mismatch\n   4e. Check for null terminator in byte 1, exit if found\n   4f. Shift EAX right 16 bits to access bytes 2-3\n   4g. Compare byte 2 (AL) with lpszStr2[2], exit if mismatch\n   4h. Check for null terminator in byte 2, exit if found\n   4i. Compare byte 3 (AH) with lpszStr2[3], exit if mismatch\n   4j. Advance both pointers by 4 bytes\n   4k. If byte 3 was not null, loop back to 4a\n5. strings_equal: Return 0 (XOR EAX, EAX)\n6. strings_differ: Compute signed result using SBB/SHL/ADD trick\n\nParameters:\n  lpszStr1 (char*) - Pointer to first null-terminated string to compare\n  lpszStr2 (char*) - Pointer to second null-terminated string to compare\n\nReturns:\n  int - Comparison result:\n    0  : Strings are identical (all bytes match including null terminator)\n    -1 : lpszStr1 is lexicographically less than lpszStr2 (unsigned byte compare)\n    +1 : lpszStr1 is lexicographically greater than lpszStr2\n\nSpecial Cases:\n  - Empty strings: If both empty, returns 0; if one empty, returns -1 or +1\n  - Uses alignment optimization: reads