      "instructions": ["0x6D21|PUSH|0x38", "0x6D23|PUSH|0x6ff757f8", "0x6D28|CALL|0x6ff539d0", "0x6D2D|XOR|EBX, EBX", "0x6D2F|CMP|dword ptr [0x6ff803dc], EBX", "0x6D35|JNZ|0x6ff56d6f", "0x6D37|PUSH|EBX", "0x6D38|PUSH|EBX", "0x6D39|XOR|ESI, ESI", "0x6D3B|INC|ESI", "0x6D3C|PUSH|ESI", "0x6D3D|PUSH|0x6ff757f4", "0x6D42|PUSH|0x100", "0x6D47|PUSH|EBX", "0x6D48|CALL|dword ptr [0x6ff75150]"],
      "comment": "CRT wrapper for LCMapString with locale-aware character mapping.\n\nConverts ANSI strings using locale-specific mapping with automatic Unicode \nconversion when the OS supports LCMapStringW. Falls back to LCMapStringA \nwith codepage conversion for older systems.\n\nAlgorithm:\n1. Check g_dwLCMapStringWSupport to determine Unicode API availability\n2. If not initialized, probe LCMapStringW with test string to detect support\n3. If Unicode supported (value 1): convert source to Unicode, map, convert back\n4. If legacy mode (value 2) or zero: use codepage conversion via ___convertcp\n5. Allocate intermediate buffers on stack or heap as needed\n6. Call LCMapStringW or LCMapStringA with locale parameters\n7. Convert result back to ANSI if using Unicode path\n8. Free any heap-allocated intermediate buffers\n9. Return number of chars written or 0 on failure\n\nParameters:\n  pLocinfo       - Locale info structure, uses g_pDefaultLocale if NULL\n  lpszLocaleName - Locale name for mapping (cast as DWORD flags)\n  dwMapFlags     - LCMapString mapping flags (LCMAP_UPPERCASE, etc.)\n  lpszSrcStr     - Source ANSI string to map\n  nCchSrc        - Source length in chars (-1 for null-terminated)\n  lpszDestStr    - Destination buffer for mapped string\n  nCchDest       - Destination buffer size in chars\n  nCodePage      - Code page for conversion (0 uses g_dwDefaultCodePage)\n  fError         - If TRUE, enables MB_ERR_INVALID_CHARS flag\n\nReturns:\n  Success: Number of characters written to destination buffer\n  Failure: 0 if mapping fails or buffers cannot be allocated\n\nSSA Temporaries (decompiler-generated, cannot be renamed):\n  bVar1, bVar2     - Boolean flags for heap allocation tracking\n  iVar3, iVar5, iVar7 - Integer results from API calls\n  DVar4            - DWORD from GetLastError\n  UVar8            - UINT from ___ansicp\n  pCVar9, pcVar14  - Character pointer temporaries\n  puVar6/10/11/12/13 - Stack/buffer pointer temporaries\n\nMagic Numbers