      "comment": "Stack probe routine for large stack allocations (Microsoft CRT).\n\nAlgorithm:\n1. Compare requested allocation size (EAX) against page size (0x1000 bytes)\n2. If allocation < one page: compute new stack pointer, probe single page, swap ESP\n3. If allocation >= one page: enter probing loop\n   3a. Save ECX (caller-saved register)\n   3b. Set probe pointer to current ESP + 8\n   3c. Subtract one page from probe pointer and allocation counter\n   3d. Touch memory at probe pointer (triggers guard page if needed)\n   3e. Repeat while remaining allocation >= one page\n4. Handle final partial page: subtract remainder from probe pointer\n5. Probe final location and commit new ESP value\n6. Restore original ECX and return address\n7. Use PUSH/RET trampoline to return to caller with adjusted stack\n\nParameters:\n  in_EAX (IMPLICIT dwAllocationSize): uint - Number of bytes to allocate on stack\n\nReturns:\n  void - Stack pointer (ESP) adjusted by requested allocation size\n  Caller's return address preserved via PUSH/RET mechanism\n\nSpecial Cases:\n  - Small allocations (<4KB): Fast path with single page probe\n  - Large allocations (>=4KB): Iterative page-by-page probing\n  - Stack overflow: Guard page violation triggers SEH exception\n\nMagic Numbers Reference:\n  0x1000 - Page size on x86 Windows (4096 bytes)\n  0x4 - Size of return address on 32-bit stack\n  0x8 - Offset to skip saved ECX and return address\n\nMemory Model:\n  This function DOES NOT follow standard calling convention for return:\n  - Uses XCHG EAX,ESP (small alloc) or MOV ESP,ECX (large alloc)\n  - Returns via PUSH EAX; RET trampoline pattern\n\nAssembly-Only Variables (Phantom):\n  local_res0 [ESP+0x0]: Return address slot\n  local_4 [ESP-0x4]: Probe target for small allocations\n  local_ffc [ESP-0xffc]: Intermediate probe during large allocations\n\nNote: Inlined function from Visual Studio 2003 Release CRT library.\nCalled by 24 functions requiring stack allocations > compiler thres