      "instructions": ["0x9668|MOV|EAX, dword ptr [ESP + 0x4]", "0x966C|CMP|EAX, dword ptr [0x6ffa7150]", "0x9672|JC|0x6ff59677", "0x9674|XOR|EAX, EAX", "0x9676|RET|", "0x9677|MOV|ECX, EAX", "0x9679|AND|EAX, 0x1f", "0x967C|SAR|ECX, 0x5", "0x967F|MOV|ECX, dword ptr [ECX*0x4 + 0x6ffa7160]", "0x9686|LEA|EAX, [EAX + EAX*0x8]", "0x9689|MOVSX|EAX, byte ptr [ECX + EAX*0x4 + 0x4]", "0x968E|AND|EAX, 0x40", "0x9691|RET|"],
      "comment": "Determines if a file handle refers to a terminal (TTY) device.\n\nChecks the FDEV flag (0x40) in the CRT IO info structure for the given file handle.\nUsed by stream buffering routines (__stbuf, __flsbuf) to determine buffering mode.\n\nAlgorithm:\n1. Validate file handle is within valid range (0 to g_nMaxFileDescriptors)\n2. If out of range, return 0 (not a terminal)\n3. Compute table segment index: nFileHandle >> 5 (divide by 32)\n4. Compute entry index within segment: nFileHandle & 0x1F (mod 32)\n5. Look up IO info table segment: g_pIoInfoTable[segmentIndex]\n6. Compute byte offset: entryIndex * 9 + 4 (each entry is 9 bytes, flags at offset 4)\n7. Read flags byte and check FDEV bit (0x40)\n8. Return non-zero if terminal device, 0 otherwise\n\nParameters:\n  nFileHandle - CRT file descriptor number (0=stdin, 1=stdout, 2=stderr, etc.)\n\nReturns:\n  Non-zero (0x40) if file handle refers to a terminal/console device\n  0 if file handle is invalid or does not refer to a terminal\n\nMagic Numbers Reference:\n  0x1F - Mask for entry index within 32-entry table segment\n  0x5  - Right shift count to get segment index (divide by 32)\n  0x9  - Size of each IO info entry in bytes\n  0x4  - Offset to flags byte within IO info entry\n  0x40 - FDEV flag indicating terminal/character device",
