      "comment": "Resets the stack guard page after a stack overflow exception.\n\nThis function is called to restore the stack's guard page protection after a\nSTATUS_STACK_OVERFLOW exception has been handled, allowing the thread to\ncontinue execution with proper stack overflow detection.\n\nAlgorithm:\n1. Query virtual memory information for the current stack pointer\n2. Get system page size via GetSystemInfo\n3. Calculate stack guard region boundaries based on OS version\n4. For NT-based systems (version != 1): scan memory regions to find committed guard page\n5. If guard page already has PAGE_GUARD protection, return success (already reset)\n6. Validate that guard region is within valid stack bounds\n7. For Win9x (version == 1): use simplified guard page positioning\n8. Allocate memory at guard page location with MEM_COMMIT\n9. Apply PAGE_GUARD protection (PAGE_READWRITE|PAGE_GUARD for NT, PAGE_NOACCESS|PAGE_GUARD for 9x)\n\nParameters:\n  None - This function takes no parameters. Uses current stack pointer implicitly.\n\nReturns:\n  1 (TRUE) - Stack guard page successfully reset\n  0 (FALSE) - Failed to reset guard page (invalid stack state or API failure)\n\nSpecial Cases:\n- OS version 1 (Win9x): Uses simplified single-page guard at stack limit\n- NT-based systems: Scans for existing committed pages and sets guard appropriately\n- Returns early success if guard page already has PAGE_GUARD attribute set\n\nMagic Numbers Reference:\n- 0x1c: sizeof(MEMORY_BASIC_INFORMATION) for 32-bit Windows\n- 0x1000: MEM_COMMIT flag for VirtualAlloc\n- 0x4: PAGE_READWRITE protection\n- 0x103: PAGE_READWRITE | PAGE_GUARD (0x100 | 0x4 - 1 = 0x103, then +1 = 0x104)\n- 0x11: Number of guard pages for NT systems (17 pages)\n- 0xfffffff1: Mask to calculate guard page count (-15, used with +17 = 2 for Win9x)\n\nError Handling:\n- VirtualQuery failure returns 0 immediately\n- Stack pointer below minimum guard region returns 0\n- Guard page address validation prevents invalid memory access",
