      "comment": "Converts a C runtime file descriptor to a Windows OS file handle.\n\nTranslates a CRT file descriptor index into the underlying HANDLE value\nthat Windows kernel APIs use. This is the inverse of _open_osfhandle.\n\nAlgorithm:\n1. Compare file descriptor against g_nMaxFileDescriptors upper bound\n2. If out of bounds, jump to error path\n3. Calculate table block index: blockIndex = nFileDescriptor >> 5 (divide by 32)\n4. Calculate entry index within block: entryIndex = nFileDescriptor & 0x1F (mod 32)\n5. Load pointer to file handle table block from g_apFileHandleTable[blockIndex]\n6. Calculate entry offset: entryIndex * 36 bytes per IOINFO structure\n7. Check if handle is valid by testing flags at offset +4 for bit 0x01 (FOPEN)\n8. If valid, return OS handle from offset +0\n9. If invalid or out of bounds, set errno to EBADF (9), clear doserrno, return -1\n\nParameters:\nnFileDescriptor (int): CRT file descriptor index (0=stdin, 1=stdout, 2=stderr, etc.)\n\nReturns:\nintptr_t: Windows HANDLE value on success, -1 (0xFFFFFFFF) on error\n\nError Handling:\n- Sets errno to EBADF (9) for invalid file descriptor\n- Clears doserrno (Win32 error) since this is not a Win32 failure\n\nStructure Layout (IOINFO - 36 bytes per entry):\nOffset  Size  Field       Description\n0x00    4     osfhnd      Windows OS file handle (HANDLE)\n0x04    1     osfile      File status flags (bit 0 = FOPEN)\n0x05    1     pipech      Pipe lookahead character\n0x06    2     padding     Alignment padding\n0x08    28    unknown     Reserved/internal fields\n\nMagic Numbers:\n0x1F = 31 - Mask for entry index within 32-entry block\n0x05 = 5 - Shift count for block index (32 entries per block)\n0x09 = 9*4=36 - Size of IOINFO structure (LEA EAX,[EAX+EAX*8] then *4)\n0x09 = EBADF - Bad file descriptor errno value",
