      "comment": "Converts a string from one Windows code page to another.\n\nThis CRT library function performs code page conversion by using the Windows\nMultiByteToWideChar and WideCharToMultiByte APIs. It handles both single-byte\nand multi-byte character encodings with an optimization path for single-byte\nto single-byte conversions.\n\nAlgorithm:\n1. If source and destination code pages are identical, return immediately\n2. Call GetCPInfo on both code pages to check if both are single-byte (MaxCharSize == 1)\n3. If both are single-byte, use direct string length calculation for buffer sizing\n4. Otherwise, call MultiByteToWideChar with NULL output to get required wide char count\n5. Allocate wide character buffer (stack if small, heap via _calloc if large)\n6. Convert source string to wide characters via MultiByteToWideChar\n7. If caller provided output buffer, convert directly to it via WideCharToMultiByte\n8. Otherwise, allocate output buffer via _calloc and perform conversion\n9. On conversion failure, free allocated output buffer via DeallocateMemory\n10. On success with length output mode (cchSrcBytes != -1), update pcchSrcBytes\n11. Free intermediate wide buffer if heap-allocated\n\nParameters:\n  dwSrcCodePage   - Source code page identifier (e.g., CP_ACP, CP_UTF8)\n  dwDstCodePage   - Destination code page identifier\n  lpszSrcString   - Pointer to null-terminated source string\n  pcchSrcBytes    - IN: Source byte count (-1 for null-terminated), OUT: Result byte count\n  lpszDstBuffer   - Output buffer, or NULL to allocate new buffer\n  nDstBufferSize  - Size of output buffer in bytes (ignored if lpszDstBuffer is NULL)\n\nReturns:\n  LPSTR - Pointer to converted string on success (either lpszDstBuffer or newly allocated)\n        - NULL on conversion failure or memory allocation failure\n\nSpecial Cases:\n  - If dwSrcCodePage == dwDstCodePage, returns immediately with no conversion\n  - If pcchSrcBytes contains -1, string is treated as null-terminated\n  - If lpszDstBuffer is NULL, function allocates buffer caller must free\n\nError Handling:\n  - Returns NULL if MultiByteToWideChar fails (invalid encoding)\n  - Returns NULL if memory allocation fails (_calloc returns NULL)\n  - Frees intermediate allocations on WideCharToMultiByte failure",
      "tags": ["PARAM_6", "CALLEE_11", "STRUCT_Act", "STRUCT_Inventory", "CALLER_2", "STRUCT_Control", "STRUCT_PlayerData"]
