      "comment": "Converts a double-precision floating-point value to exponential (scientific) notation string.\n\nThis is the CRT library function __cftoe (convert float to exponential). It formats a double value\ninto a string representation using scientific notation (e.g., \"1.234e+05\").\n\nAlgorithm:\n1. Save stack canary for buffer overflow protection\n2. Extract the double value from the pointer parameter\n3. Call __fltout2 to decompose the float into mantissa, exponent, and sign components\n4. Calculate the buffer write position accounting for sign character (0x2d = '-')\n5. Call __fptostr to convert the decomposed float to a digit string\n6. Call __cftoe2 to format the final exponential notation output\n7. Verify stack canary before returning\n\nParameters:\n  pdValue (double*) - Pointer to the double value to convert\n  szBuffer (char*) - Output buffer for the formatted string\n  nBufferSize (size_t) - Size of the output buffer in bytes\n  nDecimalPlaces (int) - Number of decimal places in the output\n  nUseCaps (int) - If non-zero, use uppercase 'E' for exponent\n\nReturns:\n  errno_t - Error code (0 on success, non-zero on failure)\n\nSpecial Cases:\n  - Negative values: Detected via sign byte 0x2d ('-'), buffer adjusted for sign character\n  - Zero precision: Handled by __cftoe2 formatting logic\n\nMagic Numbers:\n  0x2d - ASCII code for '-' (minus sign)\n\nSecurity:\n  Uses stack canary (g_dwSecurityCookie) for buffer overflow detection",
