      "comment": "Checks if all mantissa bits after a given bit position are zero.\n\nUsed during floating-point rounding to determine if truncation loses precision.\nThe mantissa is stored as an array of 3 DWORDs (96 bits total).\n\nAlgorithm:\n1. Calculate DWORD index by dividing bit position by 32\n2. Calculate bit offset within the DWORD (bit position mod 32)\n3. Create a mask for bits below the bit offset in the first DWORD\n4. Apply mask to first DWORD and check if any masked bits are set\n5. If non-zero bits found, return 0 (tail is not zero)\n6. Loop through remaining DWORDs (indices up to 2)\n7. If any subsequent DWORD is non-zero, return 0\n8. If all checked bits are zero, return 1 (tail is zero)\n\nParameters:\n    pMantissa   - Pointer to array of 3 DWORDs representing mantissa bits\n    nBitPosition - Bit position to start checking from (0-95)\n\nReturns:\n    1 - All tail bits (from nBitPosition to end) are zero\n    0 - At least one tail bit is non-zero\n\nMagic Numbers:\n    0x20 (32)  - Bits per DWORD, used for index/offset calculation\n    0x1f (31)  - Bit mask for mod 32 operation\n    0x3 (3)    - Number of DWORDs in mantissa array\n\nCalled By: RoundMantissa - to check if rounding can be exact",
