      "comment": "Converts an IEEE 754 double-precision floating-point value to a decimal string representation.\nThis is the Microsoft CRT internal function $I10_OUTPUT used for printf-style float formatting.\n\nAlgorithm:\n1. Extract sign bit from exponent field (bit 15), set output sign char to '-' or ' '\n2. Check for special values (zero, infinity, NaN):\n   - If mantissa and masked exponent are both zero, output \"0\"\n   - If exponent == 0x7FFF (max), check for INF/IND/SNAN/QNAN special strings\n3. For normal values, build 12-byte extended precision representation:\n   - Initialize LDBL12 buffer with 0xCC marker bytes\n   - Copy input mantissa and exponent into LDBL12 structure\n4. Calculate decimal exponent using log10 approximation:\n   - nDecExp = -(((exp >> 8) + (mant >> 24) * 2) * 0x4D + exp * 0x4D10 + 0xECBCED0C) >> 16\n5. Normalize value using ___multtenpow12 to shift decimal point\n6. If value >= 1.0 (exponent >= 0x3FFF), apply ___ld12mul for additional normalization\n7. Apply format flags to determine output precision:\n   - If flag bit 0 set, adjust precision based on decimal exponent\n   - Clamp precision to maximum of 21 digits\n8. Shift out integer portion via 8 iterations of ___shl_12\n9. For negative exponent, apply ___shr_12 to align decimal\n10. Extract digits by repeated multiplication and conversion:\n    - Copy LDBL12, shift left twice, add original, shift again\n    - Extract top byte, add 0x30 ('0') to convert to ASCII\n11. Perform rounding on final digit string:\n    - If last digit >= '5', propagate carry backwards\n    - If carry reaches first position, increment exponent\n12. Store final exponent and null-terminate digit string\n\nParameters:\nnMantissaLow - Low 32 bits of IEEE 754 double mantissa (bits 0-31)\ndwMantissaHigh - High 32 bits containing mantissa bits 32-51 and exponent bits 52-62\ndwExponentField - Full exponent field with sign bit (bit 15 = sign, bits 0-14 = biased exp)\nnPrecision - Requested number of significant digits for output\nbFormatFlags - Format control: bit 0 = adjust precision by exponent\npOutputStruct - Pointer to STRFLT output structure:\n  Offset 0x00: short nDecimalExponent - Base-10 exponent of result\n  Offset 0x02: char cSign - Sign character ('-' or ' ')\n  Offset 0x03: char nDigitCount - Number of significant digits\n  Offset 0x04: char[] szDigits - Null-terminated digit string\n\nReturns:\n1 - Success, output structure filled with valid decimal representation\n0 - Special case handled (INF, NaN, or zero value)\n\nSpecial Cases:\n- Zero: Sets szDigits[0]='0', nDecimalExponent=0, cSign=' ', nDigitCount=1\n- +Infinity: Copies \"1#INF\" to szDigits, nDigitCount=5\n- -Infinity: Copies \"1#INF\" to szDigits with cSign='-', nDigitCount=5\n- Quiet NaN: Copies \"1#QNAN\" to szDigits, nDigitCount=6\n- Signaling NaN: Copies \"1#SNAN\" to szDigits, nDigitCount=6\n- Indeterminate: Copies \"1#IND\" to szDigits, nDigitCount=5\n\nMagic Numbers:\n0x7FFF - Maximum exponent value (indicates special: INF/NaN)\n0x8000 - Sign bit mask in exponent field\n0x80000000 - High bit of mantissa for INF/NaN detection\n0x40000000 - QNaN indicator bit\n0xC0000000 - Indeterminate (IND) pattern\n0x4D10, 0x4D - Coefficients for log10 approximation\n0xECBCED0C - Bias constant for decimal exponent calculation\n0x3FFF - Exponent bias for extended precision (16383)\n0xCC - Padding byte for LDBL12 initialization\n\nNote: psVar1/szTempPtr is a phantom SSA variable not present in decompiled output.",
