      "comment": "Finds the position of the first clear (0) bit in a 32-bit bitmask.\n\nAlgorithm:\n1. Invert the input bitmask using NOT instruction to convert clear bits to set bits\n2. Use BSF (Bit Scan Forward) instruction to find the position of the first set bit\n3. The BSF result is the zero-based bit position (0-31) of the first clear bit in original mask\n4. Return the bit position in EAX\n\nParameters:\n   bitmask - 32-bit value in ECX where each bit represents a slot state (0=available, 1=occupied)\n\nReturns:\n   int - Zero-based bit position (0-31) of the first clear bit, stored in EAX\n\nSpecial Cases:\n   Empty bitmask (0x00000000): All bits are clear, returns 0\n   Full bitmask (0xFFFFFFFF): No clear bits exist, BSF produces undefined result; caller must validate input\n   This is a critical performance function for pool allocation: uses single NOT+BSF sequence\n\nNotes:\n   Used by pool allocation routines to find next available slot\n   Called from FindNextSlotInNodeArray to scan slot descriptors\n   Compatible with slot bit numbering: bit 0 = slot 0, bit 1 = slot 1, etc.\n   Typical usage: Check if result is valid before using as array index",
