      "comment": "Detects CPU type and displays vendor, version, and speed information.\n\nAlgorithm:\n1. Load global CPU type identifier from g_wMinVersionRequired\n2. Check if CPU type == 3 (Intel 386):\n   - If true, call ValidateParameterOrShutdown with Intel 386 string\n   - Exit function\n3. Check if CPU type == 4 (Intel 486):\n   - If true, call ValidateParameterOrShutdown with Intel 486 string\n   - Exit function\n4. For modern CPUs (type > 4):\n   a. Call GetCpuVendorId() to get CPU stepping ID\n   b. Call GetCpuVersionInfo() to retrieve CPU signature (32-bit)\n   c. Extract CPU feature fields via bit shifting:\n      - Model: (signature >> 12) & 0x3\n      - Family: (signature >> 8) & 0xf\n      - Type: (signature >> 4) & 0xf\n      - Stepping: lowest 8 bits\n   d. Call GetCpuVendorString() to get vendor string index\n   e. Call GetCpuBrandString() to format CPU info\n   f. Print vendor, version, and speed info\n\nParameters:\nNone. Reads from g_wMinVersionRequired and processor registers.\n\nReturns:\nvoid. Prints via ValidateParameterOrShutdown calls.\n\nGlobal References:\ng_wMinVersionRequired (0x6ff80b10) - CPU type (3=386, 4=486, >4=modern)\ng_pszCpuVendorStrings (0x6ff7ff78) - Vendor string lookup table\n\nSpecial Cases:\n- Values 3 and 4 are legacy Intel 386/486 CPUs\n- Stack allocation of 0x108 bytes for szCpuBrandBuffer",
