      "comment": "Encodes a byte string into a destination buffer, converting control characters to escape sequences.\nSupports optional prefix/suffix delimiter characters for quoting. Control characters below 0x20 are\nconverted to backslash-escape sequences (\\r, \n, \\t) or hex escapes (\\xNN) for other non-printables.\n\nAlgorithm:\n  1. Validate destination buffer is non-NULL (fatal error if NULL)\n  2. If source is NULL, write \"<NULL>\" literal and return\n  3. If max length is 0, return immediately with empty output\n  4. If prefix char is non-zero, write it as opening delimiter\n  5. Loop through source bytes until NULL terminator or buffer full:\n     a. If char >= 0x20 (printable): copy directly to output\n     b. If char == 0x0D (CR): write \"\\r\" escape sequence\n     c. If char == 0x0A (LF): write \"\n\" escape sequence\n     d. If char == 0x09 (TAB): write \"\\t\" escape sequence\n     e. Otherwise: write \"\\xNN\" hex escape (requires 4 bytes space)\n  6. If prefix char is non-zero, write it as closing delimiter\n  7. Null-terminate the output string\n  8. Validate output did not exceed buffer (fatal error if overflow)\n\nParameters:\n  pbyDestBuffer    [ECX] - Destination buffer to receive encoded string, must be non-NULL\n  pbySourceString  [EDX] - Source byte string to encode, NULL writes \"<NULL>\"\n  nMaxLength       [Stack+0x04] - Maximum bytes to write including null terminator\n  byPrefixChar     [Stack+0x08] - Optional delimiter char (e.g., '\"'), 0 for none\n\nReturns:\n  byte* - Pointer to pbyDestBuffer on success\n  Never returns if buffer is NULL or output overflow detected (calls ReportError)\n\nSpecial Cases:\n  - NULL source: Writes literal \"<NULL>\" string\n  - Zero max length: Returns destination buffer unchanged\n  - Control chars requiring more space than available: Encoding stops early\n  - Buffer overflow: Fatal error via LogErrorWithContext + ReportError(-1)\n\nEscape Sequence Reference:\n  0x0D (CR)    -> \\r (backslash + 'r')\n  0x0A (LF)    -> \n (backslash + 'n')\n  0x09 (TAB)   -> \\t (backslash + 't')\n  0x00-0x1F    -> \\xNN (backslash + 'x' + two hex digits)",
