      "comment": "Validates a permission mask against CIDR-based permission entries in the connection context.\nIterates through permission entries stored in the connection context, applying CIDR prefix\nmasks from a global lookup table, and checks if the masked permission matches any entry.\n\nAlgorithm:\n1. Retrieve permission entry count from context structure at offset 0x848\n2. If count <= 0, return 0 (no valid permissions)\n3. Initialize entry pointer to first permission entry at context + 0x4c\n4. For each permission entry (8-byte pairs):\n   a. Read CIDR prefix length from entry[0] (used as table index)\n   b. Look up corresponding mask from g_adwCidrPrefixMaskTable[prefixLen]\n   c. AND the mask with dwPermissionMask input\n   d. Compare result against expected value from entry[-1] (previous dword)\n   e. If match found, return 1 (permission validated)\n5. If no entries match, return 0 (permission denied)\n\nParameters:\n  in_EAX (IMPLICIT) - pConnectionContext: Pointer to connection context structure\n                      containing permission entries at offset 0x4c and count at 0x848\n  dwPermissionMask - Permission bitmask to validate against CIDR-based entries\n\nReturns:\n  uint - 1 if permission mask matches any CIDR-based entry, 0 otherwise\n\nStructure Layout (Connection Context):\n  Offset   Size   Field                    Type        Description\n  0x4c     8*N    Permission entries       struct[N]   Array of CIDR permission pairs\n           0x0    - dwCidrPrefixLength     uint        Index into g_adwCidrPrefixMaskTable\n          -0x4    - dwExpectedMaskedValue  uint        Expected value after masking\n  0x848    4      dwPermissionEntryCount   int         Number of permission entries\n\nMagic Numbers Reference:\n  0x4c   - Offset to first permission entry in connection context\n  0x848  - Offset to permission entry count in connection context\n  0x8    - Size of each permission entry (2 DWORDs)\n\nGlobal Data:\n  g_adwCidrPrefixMaskTable @ 0x6ff7fcf0 - uint[33] CIDR prefix mask lookup table\n    Index 0: 0x00000000 (/0)    Index 16: 0xFFFF0000 (/16)\n    Index 8: 0xFF000000 (/8)    Index 24: 0xFFFFFF00 (/24)\n    Index 32: 0xFFFFFFFF (/32)",
