      "comment": "Constructs a canonical Huffman code table from symbol frequencies.\n\nAlgorithm:\n1. Early exit if numSymbols is 0\n2. Special case: if numSymbols is 1, set first element to 0\n3. Merge phase: add frequencies[1] to frequencies[0]\n4. Build Huffman tree: merge pairs using two-pointer technique (nLeftIdx, nRightIdx)\n   - Compares elements from left and right, adds smaller to merged tree\n   - Uses in-place array manipulation for memory efficiency\n5. Backtracking phase: traverse merged tree to assign code bit lengths\n   - Indirect array lookup: depth[i] = depth[parent[i]] + 1\n6. Code assignment phase: generate canonical code values\n   - Counts symbols at each bit length (nSymbolCount)\n   - Fills code array with sequential values per level\n   - Doubles available codes per level (binary tree property)\n\nParameters:\n- pnCodes (EAX, IMPLICIT): Pointer to int array (modified in-place)\n  - Input: symbol frequencies at indices [0..numSymbols-1]\n  - Output: canonical code values at same indices\n- numSymbols (ECX): Number of symbols to process\n\nReturns:\n- void; pnCodes is modified with canonical Huffman code assignments\n\nLocal Variables:\n- nMergeIdx: Current merge position during tree building\n- nLeftIdx: Left pointer for two-pointer merge algorithm\n- nRightIdx: Right pointer for merge / symbol scan index\n- nSymbolCount: Count of symbols at current bit length\n- nLastIdx: Last valid index (numSymbols - 1) / output write position\n- nDepthIdx: Index for depth calculation during backtrack\n- nCodeLen: Current code length value being processed\n- nRemainSlots: Remaining code slots available at current level\n\nSpecial Cases:\n- numSymbols = 0: Early return without modification\n- numSymbols = 1: Single symbol assigned code value 0\n- numSymbols >= 2: Full canonical Huffman tree construction\n\nAlgorithm Correctness Notes:\nThis implementation uses canonical Huffman coding, which generates minimum-length\nprefixes by assigning sequential binary values to symbols grouped by code length.\nThe merge phase builds an implicit tree in-place, the backtracking phase computes\ncode lengths via parent pointer traversal, and the final phase assigns canonical\ncodes using the binary tree property (2x codes available per level).",
