      "comment": "Processes an active pool slot by advancing slot and base address counters.\n\nAlgorithm:\n1. Load and test the active flag at offset +0xC in the pool slot structure\n2. If flag is clear (zero), exit immediately without modification\n3. Load current base address from offset +0x0\n4. Load current slot index from offset +0x8\n5. Increment both slot index and base address by 1\n6. Store incremented slot index back to offset +0x8\n7. Store incremented base address back to offset +0x0\n8. Clear the active flag by setting offset +0xC to 0\n9. Calculate required capacity: (slotIndex + 1) * 8 bytes (8-byte stride)\n10. Load capacity limit from offset +0x4\n11. If calculated size is less than capacity limit, initialize byte at (baseAddress + 1) to zero\n12. Return to caller with stack cleanup (stdcall convention)\n\nParameters:\n  pPoolSlot (PoolSlot *) - Pointer to pool slot structure containing:\n    baseAddress (offset +0x0) - Current base pointer for slot data, incremented per cycle\n    capacityLimit (offset +0x4) - Maximum allocation size in bytes, controls memory init\n    slotIndex (offset +0x8) - Current slot counter, incremented per activation\n    activeFlag (offset +0xC) - Non-zero when slot should be processed, cleared on exit\n\nReturns:\n  void - No return value\n\nSpecial Cases:\n  If activeFlag is zero, the entire function exits immediately without side effects\n  Memory initialization only occurs if (slotIndex + 1) * 8 < capacityLimit\n  Uses 8-byte stride for slot sizing (typical for 64-bit pointer pairs)\n  Edge case: When slotIndex reaches capacity, initialization is skipped but counters still advance\n  This appears to be pool management code maintaining a circular or staged allocation pattern",
