      "comment": "Performs bitwise AND operation between two source buffers and stores result in destination buffer.\nOptimized to process 32-bit DWORDs at a time with byte-by-byte handling for trailing bytes.\n\nAlgorithm:\n  1. Convert bit length to byte length: nByteLength = (dwBitLength + 7) / 8\n  2. Calculate number of complete DWORDs to process: nDwordCount = nByteLength / 4\n  3. Main DWORD loop: For each complete 4-byte chunk:\n     a. Read DWORD from pdwSource1 at current offset\n     b. Read DWORD from pdwSource2 at current offset  \n     c. Perform bitwise AND: result = source1 & source2\n     d. Store result DWORD to pdwDestination at current offset\n  4. Calculate remaining trailing bytes: nTrailingBytes = nByteLength % 4\n  5. Process trailing bytes (1-3 bytes) individually via fall-through switch:\n     - 3 remaining: AND byte at offset+2, then fall through\n     - 2 remaining: AND byte at offset+1, then fall through\n     - 1 remaining: AND byte at offset+0\n  6. Return (void)\n\nParameters:\n  pdwSource1 [IN]     - Pointer to first source buffer (uint array)\n  pdwSource2 [IN]     - Pointer to second source buffer (uint array)\n  pdwDestination [OUT] - Pointer to destination buffer to receive AND result\n  dwBitLength          - Total bit length of data to process\n\nReturns:\n  void - No return value. Result written directly to pdwDestination buffer.\n\nSpecial Cases:\n  - Zero bit length: No bytes processed, returns immediately after calculation\n  - Non-DWORD-aligned lengths: Trailing 1-3 bytes handled separately\n  - All buffers must be pre-allocated with sufficient size\n\nMagic Numbers:\n  0x7 (7)   - Used for ceiling division to round up bit-to-byte conversion\n  0x3 (3)   - Used for ceiling division to round up byte-to-DWORD conversion\n  0x1f (31) - Shift count for sign extension (arithmetic right shift)\n\nMemory Access Pattern:\n  Sequential read from both source buffers, sequential write to destination.\n  DWORD-aligned accesses for main loop, byte accesses for trailing data.",
