      "comment": "Initializes a threaded subsystem by creating synchronization events and spawning a worker thread.\n\nAlgorithm:\n1. Validate that pGameState is not NULL (ESI != 0)\n2. Check that the game state has not been previously initialized (*pGameState == 0)\n3. Clear initialization state flag at offset 0x124 to 0\n4. Create an auto-reset event using CreateEventA() with default parameters (non-signaled)\n5. Store the returned event handle at offset 0x114 within pGameState\n6. Calculate the address of the thread ID field (pGameState + 0x4)\n7. Create a worker thread using CreateThread(), passing:\n   - pGameState as the context parameter\n   - ThreadWorkerProcessGameState as the thread start routine\n   - Address of thread ID field at offset 0x4 for the lpThreadId parameter\n8. Store the returned thread handle at offset 0x0 within pGameState\n9. Enter polling loop: Call WaitForSingleObject() with 0ms timeout (non-blocking)\n10. If thread has not completed (return value != 0), sleep 10ms and retry\n11. Continue polling until WaitForSingleObject() returns 0 (thread signaled/complete)\n\nParameters:\npGameState: Pointer to game state/context structure. On entry, must be non-NULL and *pGameState \nmust equal 0 (indicating uninitialized state). Structure layout:\n  Offset 0x0: HANDLE hThreadHandle - Thread handle created by CreateThread\n  Offset 0x4: DWORD dwThreadId - Thread identifier assigned by CreateThread\n  Offset 0x114: HANDLE hSyncEvent - Auto-reset event handle created by CreateEventA\n  Offset 0x124: DWORD dwInitState - Initialization state flag (cleared at start)\n\nReturns:\nvoid - No return value. Initialization is indicated through updates to pGameState structure.\n\nSpecial Cases:\n- If pGameState is NULL, function returns immediately without action\n- If *pGameState != 0 (structure already initialized), function returns immediately\n- WaitForSingleObject() timeout is 0ms (non-blocking, immediate return)\n- Sleep(10) provides 10ms polling interval between thread completion checks\n- Thread creation and synchronization are blocking operations that complete before polling starts",
