      "comment": "Table-driven dispatcher for extended-precision floating-point operations on 80-bit values.\n\nAlgorithm:\n1. Allocate 0x2c (44) bytes of stack space for local extended-precision values\n2. Extract all 8 FPU registers (ST0-ST7) and decompose each into components (low 32-bit, high 32-bit, 16-bit exponent)\n3. Mask EAX opcode selector to 6 bits (0-63) for safe table indexing\n4. Jump to appropriate handler via table at 0x6ff8005e using (opcodeSelector * 4) as offset\n5. Execute selected operation (simple FPU ops, calls to ProcessExtendedPrecisionDivision, or INT 6 for invalid cases)\n6. Return result in ST0 register (80-bit extended precision float)\n\nParameters:\n- opcodeSelector (EAX): Operation type selector (0-63); masked with 0x3f before dispatch\n- ST0-ST7: Input extended-precision floating-point operands (80-bit each) - IMPLICIT FPU stack registers\n\nReturns:\n- ST0: Result of selected operation (may be final result, NaN, or intermediate value)\n- Stack frame restored (0x2c bytes deallocated)\n\nSpecial Cases:\n- Opcodes 0,2,4,6: Self-divide operations (FDIV/FDIVR ST0,ST0) producing NaN\n- Opcodes 1,3,9,11,13,1B,23,2B,33,3B: INT 6 software interrupt for invalid operations\n- Opcodes 5,7: Direct returns of ST1 value\n- Remaining opcodes (8+): Call ProcessExtendedPrecisionDivision with various register pair combinations\n\nMagic Numbers Reference:\n- 0x3f: 6-bit mask for opcode bounds (limits to 0-63)\n- 0x2c: Stack frame size (44 bytes) for local extended-precision storage\n- 0x6ff8005e: Jump table base address (64 entries * 4 bytes = 256 bytes)\n\nGhidra Decompiler Notes:\n- in_ST0 through in_ST7: x87 FPU stack register inputs (unkbyte10 type is correct for 80-bit floats)\n- extraout_ST1, extraout_ST1_00: FPU register output variants from different code paths\n- These are Ghidra-generated intrinsic names for hardware FPU registers and cannot be renamed",
