      "comment": "Determines if a thrown exception type matches a catch handler type.\n\nThis Visual Studio runtime function is part of the C++ exception handling mechanism.\nIt checks type compatibility between a thrown exception and a catch block handler,\nconsidering type identity, cv-qualifiers (const/volatile), and inheritance.\n\nAlgorithm:\n1. Extract type descriptor pointer from catchable type (ESI + 0x4)\n2. If type descriptor is null or empty name string, match succeeds (catch-all)\n3. Compare type descriptors by pointer for fast identity check\n4. If pointers differ, compare type name strings via CompareStrings\n5. If names mismatch, return 0 (no match)\n6. Check cv-qualifier compatibility:\n   6a. If catchable is reference (bit 1) but handler not by-reference (bit 3 clear), fail\n   6b. If handler expects const (bit 0) but catchable lacks const, fail\n   6c. If handler expects volatile (bit 1) but catchable lacks volatile, fail\n7. All checks passed, return 1 (match)\n\nParameters:\n  pHandlerType (_s_HandlerType*): Catch handler type [STACK+0x4]\n    - Offset 0x0: adjectives - cv-qualifier flags\n  pCatchableType (_s_CatchableType*): Thrown exception type [IMPLICIT ESI]\n    - Offset 0x0: properties - type properties and cv-qualifiers\n    - Offset 0x4: pType - pointer to _TypeDescriptor\n  pThrowInfo (_s_ThrowInfo*): Throw information [STACK+0x8, unused]\n  IMPLICIT unaff_ESI: Points to pCatchableType for direct memory access\n  IMPLICIT unaff_EDI: Points to handler type for comparison\n\nReturns:\n  1 - Type matches: catch handler can catch this exception\n  0 - Type mismatch: cv-qualifier or type name incompatibility\n\nMagic Numbers:\n  Bit 0 (0x01): TI_CONST - const qualifier\n  Bit 1 (0x02): TI_VOLATILE - volatile qualifier\n  Bit 3 (0x08): CT_BY_REFERENCE - by-reference flag\n  Offset 0x4: Pointer to _TypeDescriptor\n  Offset 0x8: Type name string in _TypeDescriptor\n\nCalled By: FindHandler (0x6ff72efb)",
