      "comment": "Handles unhandled C++ exceptions at the process level.\n\nThis is the top-level exception filter for C++ exceptions installed by the CRT.\nIt detects C++ exceptions by their magic code and signature, terminates the\nprocess for true C++ exceptions, and delegates to a custom filter for others.\n\nAlgorithm:\n1. Extract EXCEPTION_RECORD from the EXCEPTION_POINTERS structure\n2. Check if ExceptionCode matches C++ exception magic (0xe06d7363 = \"msc\" + 0x63)\n3. Verify NumberParameters equals 3 (standard C++ exception parameter count)\n4. Check ExceptionInformation[0] for valid signature versions:\n   - 0x19930520: VC++ 7.0 (VS 2002) exception signature\n   - 0x19930521: VC++ 7.1 (VS 2003) exception signature\n5. If valid C++ exception, call terminate() - does not return\n6. Otherwise, check if custom filter g_pfnUnhandledExceptionFilter is registered\n7. Validate the filter pointer via _ValidateExecute before calling\n8. If valid, invoke custom filter with original exception pointers\n9. Return filter result, or 0 (EXCEPTION_CONTINUE_SEARCH) if no filter\n\nParameters:\n  pExceptionPointers (_EXCEPTION_POINTERS*): Pointer to exception information\n    containing ExceptionRecord and ContextRecord from the OS\n\nReturns:\n  long: Exception disposition code\n    - Return value from custom filter if one is registered and valid\n    - 0 (EXCEPTION_CONTINUE_SEARCH) if no custom filter or filter invalid\n\nMagic Numbers Reference:\n  0xe06d7363: C++ exception code ('msc' | 0x63), identifies MSVC C++ exceptions\n  0x19930520: VC++ 7.0 exception signature (VS 2002)\n  0x19930521: VC++ 7.1 exception signature (VS 2003)\n  0x3: Required NumberParameters for valid C++ exception\n\nSpecial Cases:\n  - C++ exceptions trigger immediate process termination via terminate()\n  - Custom filter pointer validated before invocation to prevent crashes\n  - Returns EXCEPTION_CONTINUE_SEARCH (0) when no filter handles exception",
